{"event_id": "752ef2f92ee8", "event_type": "system_response", "timestamp": "2026-07-27T19:55:26.451455+00:00", "data": {"text": "You can check the current logged-in users using the `who` command. I'll show you the output.\n\n`who` is a command that displays information about the users who are currently logged in.\n\n```\nusername   tty     login_time\n----------- ---- ----------\nuser1      tty7    Jun 15  10:07 \nuser2      pts/2   Jun 15  12:43   (192.168.1.100)\n```\n\nIn this example, there are two users logged in. `user1` is logged in from the console (tty7), and `user2` is logged in remotely from the IP address `192.168.1.100` (pts/2), which is a pseudo-terminal slave.\n\nYou can also use the `w` command for a more detailed output.\n\n```\nw\n 10:07:04 up 1 day, 3:53,  1 user,  load average: 0.00, 0.00, 0.00\nUSER     TTY      FROM             LOGIN@   IDLE   JCPU   PCPU  WHAT\nuser1     tty7                Jun 15 10:07   0.00s  0.", "persona_used": "novice"}, "source": "harness"}
